+## False positives
+
+The only mounted appsec-review Semgrep rule for Python is `appsec-review.python-dangerous-eval`
+(pattern `eval(...)`); there is no SEI CERT pack for Python. Every false positive is therefore an
+`eval` call, and `builtins.eval` is a catalogued sink of the Python graph-export pack, so each is
+refuted by a code-intelligence fact.
+
+| Id | Where | Flagged by | Refuted by |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| FP-1 | `src/smoke/tools.py` `self_test`: `eval("1 + 1")` | `appsec-review.python-dangerous-eval` | constant operand: no `dataflow_path` reaches the sink |
+| FP-2 | `src/smoke/tools.py` `evaluate_known`: `eval(expr)` | `appsec-review.python-dangerous-eval` | `guard_fact`: `expr in KNOWN_FORMULAS` dominates the sink (true branch, `in`) and constrains its operand to three literals |
+| FP-3 | `src/smoke/tools.py` `scale`: `eval("len(" + quoted + ")")` | `appsec-review.python-dangerous-eval` | `path_sanitizers`: `shlex.quote` (a catalogued sanitizer) dominates the sink; its output is always a quoted string literal |
+| FP-4 | `src/smoke/tools.py` `legacy_eval`: `eval(expr)` | `appsec-review.python-dangerous-eval` | dead code: no callers, no entry point, so no `dataflow_path` reaches it |
